@h1 The spec h1@

A note on where the authoritative description of Wit lives, and where it is
going.

@aside(class note) 💡 The standalone spec document in the repository is
@strong stale strong@ and is not authoritative. Do not treat it as the source of
truth for current behaviour. aside@

@h2 What is authoritative instead h2@

When two descriptions of Wit disagree, believe them in this order:

@ul
@li @strong The code strong@ — the parser, runtime, and renderers as they build
today. li@
@li @strong The fixtures strong@ — verified input-and-snapshot pairs that pin every
behaviour. li@
@li @strong The examples strong@ — whole documents that build green. li@
@li @strong The project docs strong@ — the README, plan, and changelog. li@
ul@

The old spec sits below all of these. Anything it claims must be confirmed
against a fixture or a real build before you rely on it.

@h2 Reference is the new spec h2@

The site's Reference section is built directly from code and fixtures and kept
honest by live examples. As it fills out, it supersedes the standalone spec
document: the plan is to deprecate and redirect the old spec once Reference is
complete.

@h2 Interim guidance h2@

If you must consult the old spec in the meantime, cross-check every claim. A
one-line @code wit build code@ or @code wit check code@ settles most questions
faster than reading prose that may have drifted.

@h2 The spec PDF h2@

A spec PDF also lives in the repository under its own version tag. Like the
Markdown spec, it is pending regeneration and carries the same caveat — verify
against the fixtures.

@h2 See also h2@

@a |href /docs/build/architecture/| Architecture a@ describes the pipeline the
fixtures test;
@a |href /docs/project/changelog/| Changelog a@ tracks released behaviour.
